  "Question": "If \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are non-zero real numbers such that\n\n\\[ |x|+y=3 \\]\n\nand\n\n\\[ |x|y+x^3=0, \\] then the integer nearest to \\(x-y\\) is",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-3\\)",
    "B": "\\(-1\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\)",
    "D": "\\(3\\)",
    "E": "\\(5\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The sign of \\(x\\) determines how \\(|x|\\) simplifies, so first decide whether \\(x\\) can be positive.\n\nSuppose \\(x>0\\). Then \\(|x|=x\\), and the second equation becomes\n\\[\nxy+x^3=0.\n\\]\nDividing by the nonzero number \\(x\\) gives \\(y=-x^2<0\\). The first equation is now \\(x+y=3\\), so \\(x=3-y>3\\). In particular, \\(1-x<0\\), which implies\n\\[\nx+y=x-x^2=x(1-x)<0.\n\\]\nThis contradicts \\(x+y=3\\). Hence \\(x\\) cannot be positive.\n\nSince \\(x\\ne0\\), we must have \\(x<0\\). Therefore \\(|x|=-x\\), and the first equation directly gives\n\\[\n-x+y=3,\n\\qquad x-y=-3.\n\\]\nThe requested difference is already an integer, so its nearest integer is \\(\\boxed{-3}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
